WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:03.890
You start your Spring Boot app, and the logs start scrolling.

2
00:00:03.940 --> 00:00:07.510
Beans, pools, migrations, endpoints…

3
00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:10.740
But now, how do you know what's actually running in your app?

4
00:00:11.690 --> 00:00:14.010
BootUI answers that, with just one dependency.

5
00:00:14.060 --> 00:00:20.650
It's a developer tool that embeds a console right inside your app, for Spring Boot,

6
00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:24.610
with MVC or WebFlux, and for Quarkus.

7
00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:29.370
It's local-only, it masks your secrets, and it's disabled in production.

8
00:00:30.980 --> 00:00:33.060
Open /bootui in your browser.

9
00:00:33.110 --> 00:00:36.430
The Overview shows your app at a glance:

10
00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:41.470
health, findings, and one known-findings score across every advisor.

11
00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:45.110
Start with the advisors.

12
00:00:45.160 --> 00:00:47.170
BootUI doesn't just show you data:

13
00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:48.780
it tells you what's wrong.

14
00:00:48.830 --> 00:00:53.590
Ten advisors review your architecture, REST API, Spring setup, database,

15
00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:57.280
Hibernate mappings, memory, security and dependencies.

16
00:00:57.330 --> 00:01:03.170
They run only when you ask, and rank every finding by severity, with a concrete fix.

17
00:01:03.220 --> 00:01:07.190
On the sample app, Hibernate flags HIB-FETCH-001:

18
00:01:07.240 --> 00:01:11.730
SampleOrder.customer is fetched eagerly, so every order drags its customer along.

19
00:01:11.780 --> 00:01:16.250
Pentesting checks your own endpoints against the OWASP Top 10,

20
00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:21.630
and Vulnerabilities matches your dependencies against OSV.

21
00:01:22.250 --> 00:01:24.040
Then open Live Activity:

22
00:01:24.090 --> 00:01:27.200
it shows everything your app is doing, as it happens.

23
00:01:27.250 --> 00:01:30.370
Live Flow draws your application map:

24
00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:34.250
incoming HTTP traffic, the database and the caches,

25
00:01:34.300 --> 00:01:37.280
animated as each request flows through.

26
00:01:37.330 --> 00:01:40.920
Below, every HTTP request is grouped with what it triggered.

27
00:01:40.970 --> 00:01:44.810
This request expands to the four SQL statements it ran.

28
00:01:44.860 --> 00:01:47.240
That one, to the exception it threw.

29
00:01:47.290 --> 00:01:50.390
Click Profile, and you get the full story of a request,

30
00:01:50.440 --> 00:01:53.180
correlated exactly by its trace ID.

31
00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:57.060
Then go deeper into the runtime.

32
00:01:57.110 --> 00:02:00.670
JVM Tuning reviews your JVM arguments,

33
00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:04.880
and turns a memory budget into ready-to-paste JVM options,

34
00:02:04.930 --> 00:02:07.880
for a dedicated host or for Kubernetes.

35
00:02:07.930 --> 00:02:13.850
Live Memory shows heap, non-heap and every memory pool, as the JVM runs.

36
00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:17.150
The Spring Security panel lists your filter chains,

37
00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:20.220
and explains which rules protect each endpoint.

38
00:02:20.270 --> 00:02:25.270
SQL Trace captures the statements your app runs, ranks them by time,

39
00:02:25.320 --> 00:02:27.030
and flags N+1 queries.

40
00:02:27.080 --> 00:02:31.070
The PostgreSQL panel reads what the database itself reports:

41
00:02:31.120 --> 00:02:34.230
cache hit ratio, sessions, the most expensive statements,

42
00:02:34.280 --> 00:02:36.940
index usage and autovacuum health.

43
00:02:36.990 --> 00:02:40.010
And Exceptions groups repeated failures into one entry,

44
00:02:40.060 --> 00:02:42.790
with the full stack trace and a triage status.

45
00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:44.560
And that's just the start:

46
00:02:44.610 --> 00:02:50.560
health, metrics, threads, beans, configuration, mappings, caches, traces, logs.

47
00:02:50.610 --> 00:02:52.850
BootUI ships around sixty panels.

48
00:02:54.730 --> 00:02:56.840
Now bring in your AI agent.

49
00:02:56.890 --> 00:03:02.020
Turn on the local MCP server, or install the BootUI agent skill,

50
00:03:02.070 --> 00:03:07.860
and GitHub Copilot, or any MCP client, can call the same seventy-nine tools.

51
00:03:07.910 --> 00:03:11.020
Ask your agent to fix the top Hibernate finding.

52
00:03:11.070 --> 00:03:16.450
It runs hibernate_scan, reads the remediation, and switches the association to LAZY.

53
00:03:16.500 --> 00:03:18.740
Then it scans again:

54
00:03:18.790 --> 00:03:22.780
three violations become two, verified against the running app,

55
00:03:22.830 --> 00:03:24.690
not guessed from the source.

56
00:03:26.630 --> 00:03:28.820
And when you'd rather stay in your terminal,

57
00:03:28.870 --> 00:03:33.750
the same diagnostics are one command away with the bootui CLI:

58
00:03:34.010 --> 00:03:37.630
exact JSON, ready for scripts and CI.

59
00:03:39.460 --> 00:03:41.000
One dependency.

60
00:03:41.050 --> 00:03:44.920
One running application, finally understandable.

61
00:03:44.970 --> 00:03:50.140
BootUI is free and open source, and contributions are welcome.

62
00:03:50.190 --> 00:03:54.850
Find it on GitHub, at jdubois/boot-ui, and give it a star!

